Rung 0 – First scan: Idle
 XIC(S:FS)[MOVE(0,Comp_State) ,MOVE(0,Comp_Next) ];
 Rung 1 – Idle → Purge
 EQ(Comp_State,0)XIC(Comp_Start_Req)XIC(Comp_Perm_OK)XIC(COMP_Trip_OK)MOVE(1,Comp_Next);
 Rung 2 – Purge timer (60 s)
 EQ(Comp_State,1)TON(Comp_Purge_TON,60000,0);
 Rung 3 – Purge → Startup
 EQ(Comp_State,1)XIC(Comp_Purge_TON.DN)MOVE(2,Comp_Next);
 Rung 4 – Startup timeout timer (30 s)
 EQ(Comp_State,2)TON(Comp_Start_TON,30000,0);
 Rung 5 – Startup → Running at speed
 EQ(Comp_State,2)GE(Comp_Speed,Comp_Speed_SP)MOVE(3,Comp_Next);
 Rung 6 – Startup timeout → Shutdown
 EQ(Comp_State,2)XIC(Comp_Start_TON.DN)LT(Comp_Speed,Comp_Speed_SP)MOVE(4,Comp_Next);
 Rung 7 – Shutdown timer (30 s)
 EQ(Comp_State,4)TON(Comp_Shutdn_TON,30000,0);
 Rung 8 – Shutdown → Idle
 EQ(Comp_State,4)XIC(Comp_Shutdn_TON.DN)MOVE(0,Comp_Next);
 Rung 9 – Abort: trip or stop request in states 1–3 → Shutdown
 [XIO(COMP_Trip_OK) ,XIC(Comp_Stop_Req) ]GT(Comp_State,0)LT(Comp_State,4)MOVE(4,Comp_Next);
 Rung 10 – Commit transition
 MOVE(Comp_Next,Comp_State);
 Rung 11 – Action: purge command
 EQ(Comp_State,1)XIC(COMP_Trip_OK)OTE(Comp_Purge_Cmd);
 Rung 12 – Action: run command in Startup and Running
 [EQ(Comp_State,2) ,EQ(Comp_State,3) ]XIC(COMP_Trip_OK)OTE(Comp_Run_Cmd);

Each output is written by one action rung only. Comp_Start_Req and Comp_Stop_Req are one-scan request bits cleared after
use (Pattern A1). State-based TON timers reset automatically when the state is left.
Structured Text Example — Dew Point Calculation (Structure Only)

 CAUTION — Placeholder Formula
 The correlation in this routine is a placeholder formula, not for use. It demonstrates Structured Text syntax only (Section
 0.9). Use the plant-approved correlation.

 (* Calc_DewPoint — Structured Text structure example
    Tags (REAL): T_Gas_F, P_Gas_PSIA, W_Content, Dew_Point_C, Dew_Point_F,
                 Dew_Point_HiAlarm_SP   (BOOL): Calc_Fault, Dew_Point_Hi_Alarm *)

 Calc_Fault := 0;                       (* Clear status every execution *)
 Dew_Point_Hi_Alarm := 0;

 T_Gas_C := (T_Gas_F - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0;

 IF (W_Content > 0.0) AND (P_Gas_PSIA > 0.0) THEN
     (* PLACEHOLDER FORMULA — NOT FOR USE *)
     Dew_Point_C := -40.0 + (W_Content / P_Gas_PSIA * 9500.0);
     IF Dew_Point_C > T_Gas_C THEN      (* Cannot exceed gas temperature *)
         Dew_Point_C := T_Gas_C;
     END_IF;
     IF Dew_Point_C  Dew_Point_HiAlarm_SP THEN
         Dew_Point_Hi_Alarm := 1;
     END_IF;
 ELSE
     Calc_Fault := 1;                   (* No valid measurement; Dew_Point_F holds last value *)
 END_IF;

Function Block Diagram (FBD) — PID Loop Description
In Function Block Diagram (FBD), the PID loop appears as graphical blocks with wires connecting them, similar to a P&ID. A
typical FBD pressure loop uses:
 • Input reference (IREF): the 1756-IF8 channel in floating-point mode (already in PSIG), or raw counts into an SCL block for
    scaling → SEP_PT_101.Value.
 • ALMA: analog alarm block comparing the PV to HH/H/L/LL limits with deadband and on-delay → alarm bits.

• PIDE: enhanced PID block (FBD/ST only) receiving PV and SP → CV (0–100 %).
  • FGEN (optional): function generator to characterize the control valve → linearized command.
  • Output reference (OREF): writes the command to the 1756-OF8 channel (floating-point mode scales 0–100 % to 4–20
     mA) → SEP_PCV_101 positioner.

5.4 Standard Ladder Patterns
The patterns below are the standard building blocks referenced throughout this manual. Tag names are examples; members
such as SEP_PT_101.Bad are UDT members.
Pattern A1: HMI Reset Pulse (One Scan)
HMI pushbuttons write a request bit. The PLC converts it to a one-scan pulse and clears the request so a lost HMI connection
cannot leave it set.

 Rung 0 – One-scan reset pulse
 XIC(Area.HMI_Reset)ONS(Area.Reset_ONS)OTE(Area.Reset_Pulse);
 Rung 1 – Clear the HMI request
 XIC(Area.HMI_Reset)OTU(Area.HMI_Reset);

Pattern A2: Analog Alarm with On-Delay and Deadband
The alarm activates after the PV stays above the setpoint for the on-delay and clears only when the PV falls below SP −
deadband. ALMA is the native alternative.

 Rung 0 – Clear point
 SUB(SEP_PT_101.PAH_SP,SEP_PT_101.PAH_DB,SEP_PT_101.PAH_Clr);
 Rung 1 – On-delay
 XIO(SEP_PT_101.Bad)GT(SEP_PT_101.Value,SEP_PT_101.PAH_SP)TON(SEP_PT_101.PAH_On,5000,0);
 Rung 2 – Alarm with deadband
 [XIC(SEP_PT_101.PAH_On.DN) ,XIC(SEP_PT_101.PAH)GT(SEP_PT_101.Value,SEP_PT_101.PAH_Clr) ]XIO(SEP_PT_101
 .Bad)OTE(SEP_PT_101.PAH);

Pattern A3: Bad-Quality Detection and Trip Behavior
Bad quality is derived from the channel status (under-range, over-range, connection fault) with a short filter. Each trip input is
configured to trip or not trip on bad quality (BadTrips), per the C&E philosophy. For a 1794-IE8, OverRange can be taken as
GT(SEP_PT_101_Raw,31804) (≈ 20.5 mA).

 Rung 0 – Filter channel faults (2 s)
 [XIC(SEP_PT_101.UnderRange) ,XIC(SEP_PT_101.OverRange) ,XIC(SEP_PT_101.Conn_Fault) ]TON(SEP_PT_101.Bad
 _On,2000,0);
 Rung 1 – Bad quality (connection fault is immediate)
 [XIC(SEP_PT_101.Bad_On.DN) ,XIC(SEP_PT_101.Conn_Fault) ]OTE(SEP_PT_101.Bad);
 Rung 2 – PAHH on-delay (good PV)
 XIO(SEP_PT_101.Bad)GT(SEP_PT_101.Value,SEP_PT_101.PAHH_SP)TON(SEP_PT_101.PAHH_On,1000,0);
 Rung 3 – PAHH trip (or bad quality when configured to trip)
 [XIC(SEP_PT_101.PAHH_On.DN) ,XIC(SEP_PT_101.Bad)XIC(SEP_PT_101.BadTrips) ]OTE(SEP_PT_101.PAHH_Trip);

Pattern A4: Valve Position Discrepancy (Fail to Open / Fail to Close)
PRE = full stroke time × 1.5. The same pattern detects a motor fail-to-start using XIC(Run_Cmd)XIO(Run_FB).

 Rung 0 – At commanded position
 [XIC(XV_201.Open_Cmd)XIC(XV_201.ZSO)XIO(XV_201.ZSC) ,XIO(XV_201.Open_Cmd)XIC(XV_201.ZSC)XIO(XV_201.ZSO
 ) ]OTE(XV_201.AtPos);
 Rung 1 – Discrepancy timer (15 s example)
 XIO(XV_201.AtPos)TON(XV_201.Disc_TON,15000,0);
 Rung 2 – Fail to open
 XIC(XV_201.Disc_TON.DN)XIC(XV_201.Open_Cmd)OTL(XV_201.FTO_Alm);
 Rung 3 – Fail to close
 XIC(XV_201.Disc_TON.DN)XIO(XV_201.Open_Cmd)OTL(XV_201.FTC_Alm);
 Rung 4 – Reset when at position
 XIC(Area.Reset_Pulse)XIC(XV_201.AtPos)[OTU(XV_201.FTO_Alm) ,OTU(XV_201.FTC_Alm) ];

Pattern A5: Start Permissives vs Running Trips
Permissives are required only to start. Trips are required to start and to keep running.

 Rung 0 – Start permissives
 XIC(P101.Suct_Valve_Open)XIC(P101.Seal_Flush_OK)GT(SEP_LT_101.Value,P101.Min_Lvl)OTE(P101.Perm_OK);
 Rung 1 – Running trips
 XIC(P101.STOP_OK)XIC(P101.OL_OK)XIC(SEP_Trip_OK)OTE(P101.Trip_OK);
 Rung 2 – Run command
 [XIC(P101.Start_Pulse)XIC(P101.Perm_OK) ,XIC(P101.Run_Cmd) ]XIO(P101.Stop_Pulse)XIC(P101.Trip_OK)OTE(P
 101.Run_Cmd);

Pattern A6: First-Out Capture
Records which cause tripped first. Place these rungs before the trip latch (Snippet 6, Rung 1).

 Rung 0 – PAHH first
 XIO(SEP_PAHH_OK)XIO(SEP_FO.Captured)[OTL(SEP_FO.PAHH) ,OTL(SEP_FO.Captured) ];
 Rung 1 – LAHH first
 XIO(SEP_LAHH_OK)XIO(SEP_FO.Captured)[OTL(SEP_FO.LAHH) ,OTL(SEP_FO.Captured) ];
 Rung 2 – Manual trip first
 XIO(MAN_TRIP_OK)XIO(SEP_FO.Captured)[OTL(SEP_FO.MAN) ,OTL(SEP_FO.Captured) ];
 Rung 3 – Reset with causes healthy
 XIC(Area.Reset_Pulse)XIC(SEP_Causes_OK)
 [OTU(SEP_FO.PAHH) ,OTU(SEP_FO.LAHH) ,OTU(SEP_FO.MAN) ,OTU(SEP_FO.Captured) ];

Pattern A7: Auto / Manual / Off Mode Handling with HOA
HMI mode and start/stop requests are one-scan requests cleared by the last rung. The field HOA switch in AUTO is required
for any PLC command. On first scan the pump is placed in Off.

 Rung 0 – Auto request
 XIC(P101.HMI_Auto)[OTL(P101.Auto) ,OTU(P101.Man) ];
 Rung 1 – Manual request
 XIC(P101.HMI_Man)[OTU(P101.Auto) ,OTL(P101.Man) ];
 Rung 2 – Off request or first scan
 [XIC(P101.HMI_Off) ,XIC(S:FS) ][OTU(P101.Auto) ,OTU(P101.Man) ];
 Rung 3 – Run command
 [XIC(P101.Auto)XIC(P101.Auto_Req) ,XIC(P101.Man)
 [XIC(P101.HMI_Start)XIC(P101.Perm_OK) ,XIC(P101.Run_Cmd) ]XIO(P101.HMI_Stop) ]XIC(P101.Trip_OK)XIC(P10
 1.HOA_Auto)OTE(P101.Run_Cmd);
 Rung 4 – Clear HMI requests
 [XIC(P101.HMI_Auto) ,XIC(P101.HMI_Man) ,XIC(P101.HMI_Off) ,XIC(P101.HMI_Start) ,XIC(P101.HMI_Stop) ]
 [OTU(P101.HMI_Auto) ,OTU(P101.HMI_Man) ,OTU(P101.HMI_Off) ,OTU(P101.HMI_Start) ,OTU(P101.HMI_Stop) ];

Pattern A8: Communications Heartbeat
Each controller increments a counter every second; the peer counter must change within 5 s or a communications fault is
declared.

 Rung 0 – 1 s tick
 XIO(HB.Tick_TON.DN)TON(HB.Tick_TON,1000,0);
 Rung 1 – Increment local heartbeat
 XIC(HB.Tick_TON.DN)ADD(HB.PLC_Count,1,HB.PLC_Count);
 Rung 2 – Detect peer change
 NE(HB.Peer_Count,HB.Peer_Last)MOVE(HB.Peer_Count,HB.Peer_Last)OTE(HB.Peer_Changed);
 Rung 3 – Stale timer
 XIO(HB.Peer_Changed)TON(HB.Peer_Stale,5000,0);
 Rung 4 – Heartbeat fault
 XIC(HB.Peer_Stale.DN)OTE(HB.Peer_Fault);

Pattern A9: Level Start/Stop with Hysteresis
The pump auto request latches at the high level and unlatches at the low level or on bad quality, so the pump does not cycle
around a single setpoint.

' InTouch QuickScript: PID Mode Request — SEP_PIC_101
 ' Trigger: Action scripts on the MAN and AUTO buttons of the PID faceplate
 ' Tag bindings (I/O tags to the PLC):
 '   PIC101_ManReq  -> HMI_PIC101_ManReq   (discrete, PLC clears after use)
 '   PIC101_AutoReq -> HMI_PIC101_AutoReq  (discrete, PLC clears after use)

 ' MAN button
 IF $OperatorLevel >= 1 THEN
     PIC101_ManReq = 1;
     LogMessage("SEP_PIC_101 MAN requested by " + $OperatorName);
 ENDIF;

 ' AUTO button
 IF $OperatorLevel >= 1 THEN
     PIC101_AutoReq = 1;
     LogMessage("SEP_PIC_101 AUTO requested by " + $OperatorName);
 ENDIF;

 NOTE
 A discrete pushbutton with a Set action can replace the script. Verify on bench before field use.
